+## Home Directory Knowledge Files
+
+In addition to project-specific knowledge files, you can create a **global knowledge file** in your home directory. This is useful for preferences that apply across all your projects.
+
+### Supported File Names
+
+Codebuff looks for these files in your home directory (in priority order):
+
+1. `~/.knowledge.md` (highest priority)
+2. `~/.AGENTS.md`
+3. `~/.CLAUDE.md`
+
+Only the first file found is used. File name matching is **case-insensitive**, so `~/.KNOWLEDGE.md` or `~/.Knowledge.md` will also work.
+
+### Creating a Home Directory Knowledge File
+
+```bash
+touch ~/.knowledge.md
+```
+
+Then add your global preferences:
+
+```markdown
+# User Preferences
+
+## Coding Style
+- Always use TypeScript over JavaScript
+- Prefer functional programming patterns
+- Use descriptive variable names
+- Add JSDoc comments to exported functions
+
+## Communication
+- Be concise in explanations
+- Show examples when explaining concepts
+- Ask for clarification on ambiguous requests
+
+## Tools & Frameworks
+- I prefer React over Vue
+- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
+- Prefer pnpm over npm
+```
+
+### When to Use Home Directory vs Project Knowledge Files
+
+| Home Directory (`~/.knowledge.md`) | Project (`knowledge.md`) |
+|-----------------------------------|------------------------------------|
+| Personal coding preferences | Project-specific conventions |
+| Preferred frameworks/tools | Architecture decisions |
+| Communication style | Build and deploy commands |
+| Global defaults | Team coding standards |
+
+Both files are loaded—project knowledge files add to (and can override) your home directory preferences.
+
